WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.700
The time is coming when the human race

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:02.540
will get to the moon,

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:03.140
with rockets!

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.340
Private companies won't want to do this,

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:08.300
how are they gonna make money?

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:09.900
By charging for the ride.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.680
Those crazy billionaires competing with each other.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.440
Modern day rocket man wants to send you to Mars.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:17.200
Difficult.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:18.360
Dangerous.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.160
Good chance you will die.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.960
The private industry's space race

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:24.240
it's on.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:31.800
We tend to think that space exploration and

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.360
human space flight is all about

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.440
governmental agencies like NASA.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.442
But it wasn't really always like that.

00:00:39.442 --> 00:00:41.980
Some of the very first steps by

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:45.840
the early rocketry pioneers 
were funded by the private sector.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:49.180
In the U.S. it begins with 
the University of Clark professor

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:50.160
Robert Goddard.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.420
It’s 1920 and he publishes

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.740
"A Method of Reaching Extreme Altitudes”

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:56.260
mentioning that rockets could be used

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.200
to carry payloads to the moon.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.800
They called him the "Moon Man" and laughed.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.900
The Times editorial writer

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.340
implied that your husband didn’t even know 
high school physics.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:10.440
He did not let such things deter him from
his experiments.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.540
In 1926 Goddard successfully launches the
first liquid-fueled rocket.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:19.300
Money from the family of the mining magnate
Daniel Guggenheim soon follows.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.100
They provided him funding in today's dollars

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.100
at the scale of tens of millions of dollars.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:29.720
What's interesting is that at the very start
of this period of the 1920s

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.980
there was actually a great amount of enthusiasm

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:33.100
and a great amount of belief

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.500
in the potential for these private rockets to go to space.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.580
The German war machine gets under way.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.060
The Second World War gives rockets a whole
new meaning.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:50.060
And then the knowledge of rocketry 
became critical to the Cold War.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:54.760
And in the ‘50s and ‘60s rocketry was almost 
exclusively a governmental affair.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:59.920
The private sector, in terms of companies,
was essentially working as contractors to

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.140
the U.S. government.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:07.220
It’s not until 1982, that a serious effort
is made to build a privately funded rocket.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:10.920
The idea comes from a Texas businessman, 
David Hannah Jr.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.940
Conestoga 1 is pieced together from a 
repurposed ballistic missile.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:21.920
It may not have had the majesty of an Apollo, 
or the thunder of a Shuttle

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:23.940
but it flew and that’s what counts.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.800
Everything speeds up at the turn of the millennium

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.380
when freshly minted millionaires take interest.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:32.160
One of them is Jeff Bezos.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.600
I don’t actually hold out great hopes.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.160
But If I could do anything,

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:39.540
I would like to go help explore space.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.400
A few months later he sets up Blue Origin.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.560
Then Elon Musk starts SpaceX.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.220
And yet another tech millionaire

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.160
Anousheh Ansari and her family

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.340
promise to foot the bill for XPrize.

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:55.560
This is a $10 million award for reaching the
industry’s holy grail -

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.600
a reusable manned space vehicle.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.560
In 2004 SpaceShipOne wins the prize.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.660
There it is, a craft that has been to space
and back, today right in front of you.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.219
Richard Branson buys the rights, 
turns it into Virgin Galactic

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:17.860
and in the same year starts accepting commercial
reservations for future suborbital flights.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:22.900
Although we are seeing a lot of billionaires 
have interest in space flight,

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:25.640
their investments still pale in comparison to governmental investments.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.560
And they have now managed to partner in many
cases with U.S. government agencies.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.680
NASA for example is in most years 
SpaceX's primary customer.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:36.960
But there are setbacks.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:41.700
Local company's mission for NASA 
explodes into a ball of fire and debris.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:45.460
Deadly crash involving Virgin Galactic’s
SpaceShipTwo.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:49.820
A SpaceX rocket bringing supplies for 
the International Space Station

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:51.540
as you can see there, it exploded!

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.520
Eventually, in 2015 Blue Origin’s New Shepard rocket

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.180
becomes the first to take off and land vertically 
after reaching space.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:08.800
Shortly after SpaceX goes even further, sending
its Falcon 9 to orbit altitude.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:15.420
The other major milestone will be the first
tourists on commercial suborbital vehicles.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:18.300
And I'm not gonna predict who's gonna be there first but

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.560
the hope is that by having

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.120
more competition amongst commercial companies

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.560
and by leveraging advanced technologies that
we're gonna be able to

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.920
reduce the cost of going into space.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:34.540
2019 is the year that SpaceX and Boeing are
gearing up to finally take astronauts

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.720
up to the International Space Station.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:39.520
The first private lunar lander is on its way
to the Moon

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.620
and Chinese companies are joining the space
race as well.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:50.160
For well over a hundred years, people have been
dreaming and thinking and planning

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.080
for a future for humanity beyond this planet.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:57.600
And that includes a future for humanity on
the surface of the moon,

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.780
a future for humanity on Mars

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.700
and even farther still.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:10.900
And as long as we have a social goal of continuing
to expand our knowledge of the cosmos,

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:13.700
then the ambitions and dreams of individuals

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:16.840
are gonna be an important part of realizing those goals.

